Subject: [PATCH 4/6] glm_routed_expert: golden asserts a lane order full_sort=False does not promise

`GLMRoutedExpert.golden` reads `top_indices[0, lane]` — it assumes output lane
`i` holds the i-th largest expert. `GLMMoERouter` runs `GenericMoeGate` with
`full_sort=False`, and that mode promises the top-k *set* and nothing about
order or tie-breaking. `test_generic_moe_gate.py:252-258` already compares
sorted sets for exactly this reason; only the `full_sort=True` branch at
`:229-241` asserts descending order. The routed-expert golden is the one place
in the family that forgot.

HOW IT SURFACED (measured, Blackhole Galaxy, GLM-4.7-Flash dims, k=4)

With patch 1 of 6 applied the gate selects real experts, and the test then
failed on a tie: experts 44 and 35 both scored exactly `1.0234375` — the bf16
value at the k-th rank cutoff. Torch's `topk` broke the tie toward one, the SFPU
toward the other. Both selections are correct. bf16 has ~8 bits of mantissa and
64 post-sigmoid scores in (0, 1); exact ties at the cutoff are not a freak
event, they are the expected case at this width.

This is a GOLDEN defect, not a device defect. Nothing was wrong on hardware. It
is worth fixing rather than working around because any future numerics gate on
this op hits it, and the natural reaction — loosening the PCC threshold — hides
real errors while not actually fixing the tie.

THE FIX

`golden_lane_candidates()` returns every output a correct lane may produce:
the strict top-(k - ties) winners plus each way the tied cutoff can be resolved,
with each candidate carrying its own routing weight. The test asserts membership
instead of equality.

Carrying the score per candidate matters. A PCC comparison is scale-invariant,
so comparing against a single candidate cannot tell whether the device paired
the right weight with the right expert; pairing each candidate output with the
score that expert would have earned restores that check. That is not
hypothetical — see the `scaling_factor` fix in the same hunk below.

ALSO IN THIS PATCH, a fifth instance of patch 2 of 6's bug class, this one in the
test: it passed `"routing_scaling_factor"` in `user_args`, but
`GLMRoutedExpert.compose` reads `"scaling_factor"`. The key was never consumed,
so the device ran the 2.5 default while the golden used GLM-5's real factor.
PCC's scale invariance is precisely why nobody noticed for the life of the test.

`golden()` is kept and now delegates, with its ordering assumption documented as
a caveat and valid only under `full_sort=True`. It also now raises on
`device_id >= num_selected_experts` rather than returning a silently wrong
reference — on device that same condition reads a `zero_tail` lane and yields
expert 0 scaled by 0 (see patch 2 of 6's UNRESOLVED note).

STATUS: VERIFIED on silicon — this is the change that took the GLM-4.7-Flash
`glm_routed_expert` probe from failing on the 44-vs-35 tie to passing. The GLM-5
k=8 path is unchanged in outcome (it happens to come out ordered) and was
re-run.

Apply after patch 3 of 6.
